            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Sprinkle water on several areas of the slab. If it beads or sits on the surface, a sealer is still present and a new coat will not penetrate. If it darkens the concrete and soaks in within about thirty seconds, the surface is open. Test several spots, because sealer wears unevenly and the sheltered areas often still have coverage when the exposed ones do not.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you need to etch old concrete before sealing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually not. Etching exists to open the closed, glassy surface of new troweled concrete, and a weathered slab is already open. Old concrete needs thorough cleaning and degreasing instead. The exception is a slab with a hard, polished, or previously power-troweled surface that still repels water after cleaning, where an acid etch or mechanical profiling is warranted.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best sealer for old concrete?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most weathered exterior slabs, a penetrating silane-siloxane sealer is the right choice: it soaks into the pores and repels water from within, leaves no film to peel, and is unaffected by UV. Film-forming acrylics give a wet look and colour enhancement but sit on the surface, so on an old slab with any residual contamination they are far more likely to peel.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long should old concrete dry before sealing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At least 24 hours after cleaning, and 48 or more if the weather is cool or humid or the slab was pressure-washed hard. Concrete holds water far longer than the surface suggests. Tape a square of plastic sheet to the slab for a few hours: condensation underneath means it is still too wet to seal.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you seal spalling or flaking concrete?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Sealing does not repair spalling — it seals whatever surface is there, including the loose material. Remove everything friable first with a scraper, wire brush, or grinder. If the sound surface underneath is acceptable, seal it; if spalling covers a large area, a resurfacer or an overlay is the correct repair, and sealing comes after that has cured.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you have to remove old sealer before applying new sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If the old sealer is a film-forming acrylic and it is peeling, cloudy or flaking, yes — strip it, because a new coat over failing film fails with it. If the old sealer is the same product, still sound, and simply worn thin, a compatible recoat is normally fine. Penetrating sealers leave nothing to strip; they are simply reapplied once the surface takes water again.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it worth sealing a very old driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If the slab is structurally sound — no heaving, no wide moving cracks, no large spalled areas — then yes, and it is one of the highest-value maintenance jobs available. Sealing slows freeze-thaw damage and de-icing salt attack, which are what actually destroy old concrete. If the slab is already breaking up, sealing preserves the damage rather than the driveway.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
