        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          The instinct that old concrete is "too far gone to seal" gets it backwards. A twenty-year-old slab has finished shrinking, its pores are wide open, and a penetrating sealer soaks into it far more readily than into a freshly troweled surface. The work is in finding out what is already on the slab and getting it genuinely clean.
        </p>

        <h2 id="can-you" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. Can You Seal Old Concrete?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Yes — and for a penetrating sealer, aged concrete is close to ideal. New concrete has to cure for at least 28 days before it can take a sealer at all, and a power-troweled surface is dense and closed enough that many products need an acid etch before they will absorb. Old concrete has none of those problems. Years of weathering have opened the surface, carbonation has run its course, and the slab has done nearly all the shrinking it will ever do.
        </p>

        <p>
          There are four conditions. The slab must be <strong>structurally sound</strong> — settled, heaved or badly cracked concrete is a repair job, not a sealing job. It must be <strong>free of any previous film-forming sealer</strong>, or the new product cannot get in. It must be genuinely <strong>clean and degreased</strong>, because oil and dirt block absorption exactly the way an old sealer does. And it must be <strong>dry</strong>, which takes far longer than people expect.
        </p>

        <p>
          Meet those four and the payoff is substantial. What destroys concrete in cold climates is water getting into the pores and freezing, and de-icing salts accelerating the same damage — the mechanisms are covered in our guide to <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">ice melt and concrete</a>. A sealer that keeps water out of the pore structure directly slows both.
        </p>

        <h2 id="assess" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Assess the Slab Before You Buy Anything
        </h2>

        <p>
          Walk the whole slab before you spend money. Five things decide whether this is a sealing job, a repair job, or a resurfacing job.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Spalling and Flaking</h3>
        <p>
          Spalling is the shallow flaking of the top layer, usually caused by freeze-thaw cycling or salt damage. Scrape a suspect area with a screwdriver: if flakes lift easily, that material has to come off before anything goes on. Sealing over loose surface simply seals loose surface. Small isolated patches can be removed and the slab sealed regardless; widespread spalling points towards a resurfacer.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cracks</h3>
        <p>
          Fine, stable hairline cracks can be sealed over with a penetrating product without concern. Anything wider than about an eighth of an inch should be filled first, and cracks with a height difference across them, or that have visibly moved over time, need investigating rather than filling. Our <a href="/driveway-crack-repair/">driveway crack repair guide</a> covers the filling methods and which cracks are worth worrying about.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Oil and Grease</h3>
        <p>
          Old driveways and garage floors carry decades of oil, and it goes deeper than it looks. A sealer will not bond or penetrate through it, and the contaminated area will show as a permanent patch. Degrease properly with a dedicated cleaner or poultice; our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers the methods that work on old stains.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Efflorescence and Mineral Staining</h3>
        <p>
          The white bloom that appears on old slabs is deposited salt from water moving through the concrete. Brush it off dry and note where it was — a slab with persistent efflorescence has water moving through it from below, which will also work against a film-forming sealer applied on top.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Moisture From Underneath</h3>
        <p>
          Tape a two-foot square of plastic sheet tightly to the slab and leave it overnight. Condensation on the underside in the morning means moisture is migrating up through the concrete. That rules out film-forming sealers — vapour pressure will lift them — and points firmly at a breathable penetrating product.
        </p>

        <h2 id="test-for-sealer" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Testing for an Existing Sealer
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the step that most often gets skipped and most often causes the failure. Old concrete has frequently been sealed at some point, and residual sealer prevents a new coat from penetrating or bonding.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The water test.</strong> Sprinkle water on half a dozen spots across the slab. Water that beads or sits on the surface means sealer is still present. Water that darkens the concrete and disappears within about thirty seconds means the surface is open and ready. Test several locations: sealer wears unevenly, and the sheltered edges often still have coverage when the traffic lanes do not.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Identifying the type.</strong> If water beads, you need to know whether you are dealing with a film-forming acrylic or a penetrating product. Dab a small area with acetone or xylene on a rag and leave it a minute. If the surface becomes tacky or the coating softens and comes away, it is a solvent-based acrylic. If nothing happens but water still beads, it is more likely a silane-siloxane that has not yet worn out — in which case the slab is already protected and does not need doing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>What to do about it.</strong> A sound, worn acrylic can be recoated with a compatible acrylic. A peeling, cloudy, or flaking acrylic has to be stripped — our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-old-driveway-sealer/">removing old driveway sealer</a> covers chemical strippers and mechanical options. A penetrating sealer that still works needs nothing; when it stops working, it is simply reapplied, with no stripping at any point. That absence of a stripping cycle is the strongest long-term argument for penetrating products on a slab you intend to keep maintaining.
        </p>

        <h2 id="which-sealer" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Which Sealer for an Old Slab
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Penetrating Silane-Siloxane — the Default</h3>
        <p>
          For most weathered exterior concrete this is the right answer. Silane-siloxane sealers react inside the pore structure and line the pores with a hydrophobic layer, so water is repelled from within rather than blocked by a film on top. Nothing sits on the surface, so there is nothing to peel, nothing to go cloudy, no UV degradation, and no change in appearance or traction. On an old slab, where perfect cleanliness across every square foot is realistically unachievable, that tolerance matters. Our <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane comparison</a> explains where the two chemistries differ.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Film-Forming Acrylic — Only With Good Reason</h3>
        <p>
          Acrylics build a film that gives the wet look, deepens colour, and adds a modest amount of abrasion resistance. On decorative, stamped or exposed-aggregate concrete that is genuinely the point, and there is no substitute. But on an old slab an acrylic is holding on to a surface that may still carry traces of oil, dust or old product, and every one of those is a place the film can lift. It is also vulnerable to moisture pushing up from below. Choose it for appearance on a sound, clean, dry slab, and accept the maintenance cycle that comes with it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Densifiers — the Interior Option</h3>
        <p>
          Lithium or sodium silicate densifiers react with free lime in the concrete to harden and dustproof the surface. On an old interior slab that generates dust — a basement or garage floor — a densifier followed by a penetrating sealer is an excellent combination. Outdoors, a densifier alone provides limited water repellency, so it is a complement rather than a substitute.
        </p>

        <div class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl">
          <h2 id="sealer-table" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-8 border-none text-center">Sealer Types on Old Concrete</h2>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border-collapse">
