                  <th class="py-3 px-4 text-[#38BDF8] font-bold text-base">On a Weathered Slab</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-4 text-[#FF4500] font-bold text-base">Lifespan</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-300">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Silane-Siloxane</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Best all-round choice</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">5-10 years</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Acrylic (solvent)</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-yellow-400">Wet look, but peels if prep is imperfect</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">2-4 years</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Acrylic (water-based)</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-yellow-400">Lower odour, shorter life</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">1-3 years</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-800">
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Silicate Densifier</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">Excellent on dusty interior slabs</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Permanent</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white">Epoxy / Urethane</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">Interior only — traps moisture outdoors</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">5-10 years indoors</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="the-method" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. The Method, Step by Step
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Remove Everything Loose</h3>
        <p>
          Scrape and wire-brush any spalled or flaking material until you reach sound concrete. A stiff broom will not do this; a floor scraper and, on stubborn areas, a grinder with a dust shroud will. Vacuum or blow the debris off rather than washing it into the joints.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Degrease, Then Clean</h3>
        <p>
          Treat oil stains first, individually, with a degreaser or poultice — washing the whole slab first just spreads them. Once the stains are dealt with, clean the full surface with a concrete cleaner. A pressure washer at moderate pressure and a fan tip does the job well; keep the tip moving and do not concentrate on one spot, because on old concrete a turbo nozzle held close will erode the surface paste and expose aggregate.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Fill the Cracks</h3>
        <p>
          Fill anything wider than a hairline with a suitable crack filler or patching compound, and let it cure for the full time on the label before sealing. Patch material that is still curing will off-gas moisture under a film-forming sealer and blister it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Let It Dry — Properly</h3>
        <p>
          A minimum of 24 hours, and 48 or more after a hard pressure-wash or in cool, humid weather. Use the plastic sheet test again if you are unsure. This is where most jobs go wrong: the surface looks dry hours before the pore structure is, and a sealer applied over damp concrete will either fail to penetrate or, with a film-former, go cloudy and blister.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Test One Small Area</h3>
        <p>
          Apply the product to a square yard somewhere inconspicuous and leave it a day. On an old slab this tells you two things nothing else will: whether the surface absorbs evenly, and what colour the concrete goes. Film-forming sealers in particular can darken weathered concrete dramatically, and finding that out on a test patch is considerably better than finding it out on a whole driveway.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Apply Thin, in Two Coats</h3>
        <p>
          Use a pump sprayer for penetrating sealers, working in overlapping passes and back-rolling if the product directs it. Two thin coats always outperform one heavy one — with a penetrating sealer the second coat goes on before the first has fully dried, and with an acrylic it goes on after the recoat window opens. Do not flood the surface: excess penetrating sealer that cannot soak in will dry as a white haze, and excess acrylic goes milky.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pro Tip: Watch the Weather Behind You, Not Just Ahead</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
            Everyone checks the forecast for rain after sealing. Fewer people account for the rain that fell three days <strong>before</strong>, which is still inside an old slab even though the surface has been dry since. On a thick or shaded slab, allow a full dry spell of two or three days before you seal, not just a dry morning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="when-to-resurface" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. When Resurfacing Beats Sealing
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sealing preserves the surface you have. If that surface is already failing, sealing preserves the failure. Three signs point to resurfacing instead.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Widespread spalling.</strong> If more than roughly a quarter of the slab has lost its top layer, patching individual areas will look like patches forever. A cement-based resurfacer applied over the whole slab gives a uniform new wearing surface — and then gets sealed once it has cured.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Exposed aggregate through wear.</strong> Where the cement paste has eroded and the stones stand proud across large areas, the slab has lost its surface. A sealer will soak in and do no harm, but it will not restore anything.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Structural movement.</strong> Sections at different heights, cracks that open and close seasonally, or corners that have dropped are foundation and subgrade issues. Nothing applied to the top fixes them, and our <a href="/driveway-resurfacing-cost/">driveway resurfacing cost guide</a> sets out what the alternatives actually cost before you commit either way.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes Sealing Old Concrete</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Testing for an Existing Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thirty seconds with a cup of water answers the question that determines the whole job. New sealer over old film sits on the surface, dries unevenly, and peels — and the product gets blamed for a test that was never done.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Oil Stains</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil blocks penetration and adhesion, and it goes deeper into old concrete than the visible stain suggests. Degrease the stains individually before cleaning the slab, and expect stubborn ones to need a poultice and a second attempt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying to Concrete That Is Still Wet Inside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dry-looking surface is not a dry slab. Penetrating sealers cannot get into pores already full of water, and film-formers trap the moisture and turn milky. Tape down a square of plastic overnight before you commit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flooding It On for Better Coverage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                More product does not mean more protection. Penetrating sealer that cannot absorb dries as a white haze on the surface; acrylic applied too heavily goes cloudy and stays soft. Two thin coats, every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Turbo Nozzle on Old Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A rotating nozzle held close will cut into aged surface paste and leave permanent wand marks and exposed aggregate. Use a wide fan tip, keep it moving, and stand back — the cleaning is chemical as much as mechanical.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal old concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, and it is often a better candidate than new concrete: a cured slab has finished shrinking and its surface is open and porous, so a penetrating sealer soaks in readily. It has to be structurally sound, thoroughly clean, free of any previous film-forming sealer, and properly dry.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know if old concrete has been sealed before?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Sprinkle water on several spots. Beading means sealer is still present and a new coat will not penetrate; water that darkens the slab and soaks in within about thirty seconds means the surface is open. Test several areas — sealer wears unevenly, and sheltered edges often still have coverage.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to etch old concrete before sealing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Usually not. Etching opens the closed, glassy surface of new troweled concrete, and a weathered slab is already open — thorough cleaning and degreasing is what it needs. The exception is a hard, polished or power-troweled surface that still repels water after cleaning, which warrants an acid etch or mechanical profiling.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sealer for old concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A penetrating silane-siloxane for most weathered exterior slabs: it soaks into the pores, repels water from within, leaves no film to peel and is unaffected by UV. Film-forming acrylics give a wet look and colour enhancement but sit on the surface, where residual contamination on an old slab makes peeling far more likely.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should old concrete dry before sealing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">At least 24 hours after cleaning, and 48 or more in cool or humid weather or after a hard pressure-wash. Concrete holds water long after the surface looks dry — tape a square of plastic sheet down for a few hours, and condensation underneath means it is still too wet.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal spalling or flaking concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Sealing does not repair spalling — it seals whatever is there, loose material included. Remove everything friable first with a scraper, wire brush or grinder. If the sound surface underneath is acceptable, seal it; if spalling is widespread, resurface first and seal once that has cured.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to remove old sealer before applying new?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">If the old sealer is a film-forming acrylic that is peeling, cloudy or flaking, yes — a new coat over failing film fails with it. A sound acrylic that is simply worn thin can take a compatible recoat. Penetrating sealers leave nothing to strip and are simply reapplied when the surface starts absorbing water again.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth sealing a very old driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">If the slab is structurally sound, yes — it is one of the highest-value maintenance jobs there is, because sealing slows the freeze-thaw and de-icing salt damage that actually destroys concrete. If the slab is already breaking up, sealing preserves the damage rather than the driveway.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

