        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Sealing old concrete</strong> is mostly a diagnostic exercise followed by a straightforward application. Test with water to find out whether an old sealer is still there. Deal with oil, loose material and cracks individually. Let the slab dry far longer than seems necessary. Then put on two thin coats of a penetrating silane-siloxane, which is the product least likely to be defeated by whatever the slab has been through.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Reserve film-forming acrylics for decorative concrete where the wet look is the whole point and the slab is clean, sound and dry enough to hold one. And if the surface has already gone — widespread spalling, exposed aggregate, sections at different heights — put the sealer back on the shelf, because that slab needs resurfacing, not protecting.
          </p>
        </div>

